{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779211840,"nonce":"oXnp1b00vfQJSdWMDVoKPMhGaOc=","message":"## Cross-Reference: value/expression specs as a core Feedbax contract\n\nNew Studio spec `bd5aefb` has core Feedbax consequences: task timelines, function-valued parameters, sampled values, and expression-backed component properties cannot remain UI-local strings if Studio is going to lower specs into backend execution and manifests.\n\nCore contract implications:\n\n- Feedbax needs a typed value/expression representation covering constants, references, structured expressions, reusable functions, schedules, and distributions.\n- Units, shape, dtype, axis/frame metadata, sampling scope, and seed policy should be part of the durable spec where relevant.\n- Backend validation should report spec-path errors usable by Studio, not just fail during training/eval execution.\n- Task generation (`c540499`, `d64a9aa`, `3fc3e8e`) should be able to consume compact declarative timeline/signal specs rather than pre-materialized arrays.\n- Provider/manifests (`c6c6da0`, `f68cf66`, `e33f487`) should preserve the exact value/function/distribution specs used for runs, evals, analyses, and reports.\n- Acausal/component editors (`a6efafc`, `6116155`) should reuse the value editor, but the core must preserve unit/domain/equation semantics rather than treating values as arbitrary text.\n\nThis is the core-library counterpart to the Studio UX point: the UI can hide JSON-ish details only if Feedbax has a real schema and lowering path underneath it.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}